--- what you built ---
  8 chips, 64 MB usable.
  fastest bank: 44.7 ns per nibble. The FlexSPI threshold is 20.2.
  banks under that threshold: 0/8
  None under 20.2 yet, so the bit-banged driver is the path for now. The
  lever is a ground return beside the data lines, not the pad drive:
  psram_pads measured all eight drive and slew settings as identical.

  Bring-up complete, but stage 3's settings are PROVISIONAL: they were chosen
  in seconds and this bus loses margin over minutes as it warms. Soaking every
  bank from here on, stepping any that fails and resetting its count, until the
  settings are ones that survive being run hard. That is the real answer.
  bank 0: 4262179 errors under load at write 5/read 14, burst 64 -- stepping to write 5/read 16, burst 64, counters reset
  bank 1: 3600312 errors under load at write 4/read 14, burst 64 -- stepping to write 4/read 16, burst 64, counters reset
  bank 2: errors at the slowest setting in the table. Slowing down is not
    the fix here -- run psram_addr_fault to tell a wire from timing.
  bank 3: errors at the slowest setting in the table. Slowing down is not
    the fix here -- run psram_addr_fault to tell a wire from timing.
  bank 4: errors at the slowest setting in the table. Slowing down is not
    the fix here -- run psram_addr_fault to tell a wire from timing.
